  "dosing": false,
  "formula": "Les estimations de survie à 5 ans sont calculées à partir des données radiologiques préopératoires (nombre de nodules et diamètre du plus grand nodule) et du taux d'AFP, et sont en outre stratifiées selon le statut d'infection par le HCV.\n\nCe modèle limite à 10 la somme du diamètre du plus grand nodule (cm) et du nombre de nodules.",
  "how_to_use": {
    "why": "- Predicts 5-year HCC-specific survival post liver transplantation.\n- Captures both tumor morphology (largest viable nodule size, number) and biology (AFP).\n- Demonstrates better prognostic discrimination than the [Milan Criteria](nabda:calc.milan-criteria-liver-transplantation) .",
    "when": "- Utilisation chez les patients adultes (âge ≥18 ans) atteints d’un carcinome hépatocellulaire (CHC) lors de l’évaluation préalable à l’inscription ou de la réévaluation de la candidature à une transplantation hépatique.\n- Utile pour évaluer les patients en dehors des [critères de Milan](nabda:calc.milan-criteria-liver-transplantation) qui pourraient tout de même obtenir des résultats acceptables.",
    "pearls": "- Derived from a multicenter Italian training cohort (n=1018) across 3 tertiary centers with external validation in Shanghai; developed amid effective anti-HCV therapies to focus on HCC-specific (cancer-related) mortality via a competing-risks approach.\n- The derivation cohort was predominantly male, with a median age of 56.6 years and median [MELD](nabda:calc.model-end-stage-liver-disease-meld) of 11.\n- At any pre-transplant time point, only nodules with a maximum diameter ≥1 cm were counted as HCC if they met imaging criteria.\n- HCC-related death was defined as death related to post-LT HCC recurrence, disseminated extra-/intrahepatic relapse, or liver failure consequent to tumor spread."
  },
  "next_steps": {
    "advice": "- Discuss results in a multidisciplinary transplant board and in shared decision-making with the patient (prognostic framing, trade-offs, and waiting-list risks).\n- Authors proposed a ~70% predicted 5-year HCC-specific survival as a pragmatic threshold for considering listing/activation when otherwise appropriate; follow local policy.",
    "critical_actions": "Allocation rules still govern transplant candidacy; this tool does not override OPTN/UNOS policy.",
    "management": ""
  },
